    "prompt": "compare slaes data of our data and seattle store.",
    "title": "Sales Data Comparison",
    "summary": "Compare our sales data with the Seattle store\u2019s sales data. Highlight key differences, trends, and any notable performance gaps.",
    "timestamp": "2026-10-01 06:54:11",
    "executed_sql_queries": [
        "SELECT\n  s.store_id,\n  s.store_name,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id) AS number_of_orders,\n  COALESCE(SUM(oi.total_amount), 0) AS total_sales_amount,\n  COALESCE(SUM(oi.quantity), 0) AS total_units_sold,\n  CASE\n    WHEN COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id) = 0 THEN 0\n    ELSE COALESCE(SUM(oi.total_amount), 0) / COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id)\n  END AS average_order_value\nFROM retail.stores s\nLEFT JOIN retail.orders o\n  ON s.store_id = o.store_id\nLEFT JOIN retail.order_items oi\n  ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\n AND o.store_id = oi.store_id\nGROUP BY s.store_id, s.store_name\nORDER BY s.store_name;",
        "WITH monthly_sales AS (\n  SELECT\n    DATE_TRUNC('month', o.order_date)::date AS month,\n    s.store_name,\n    SUM(oi.total_amount) AS sales_amount\n  FROM retail.orders o\n  JOIN retail.order_items oi\n    ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\n   AND o.store_id = oi.store_id\n  JOIN retail.stores s\n    ON o.store_id = s.store_id\n  GROUP BY 1, 2\n)\nSELECT\n  month,\n  CASE\n    WHEN store_name = 'Zava Retail Seattle' THEN 'Zava Retail Seattle'\n    ELSE 'All accessible stores overall'\n  END AS comparison_group,\n  SUM(sales_amount) AS sales_amount\nFROM monthly_sales\nGROUP BY 1, 2\nORDER BY 1, 2;",
        "SELECT c.category_name,\n       SUM(oi.total_amount) AS sales_amount,\n       SUM(oi.quantity) AS units_sold,\n       COUNT(DISTINCT o.order_id) AS order_count\nFROM retail.orders o\nJOIN retail.order_items oi\n  ON o.order_id = oi.order_id\nJOIN retail.products p\n  ON oi.product_id = p.product_id\nJOIN retail.categories c\n  ON p.category_id = c.category_id\nGROUP BY c.category_name\nORDER BY sales_amount DESC;",
        "SELECT COUNT(*) AS seattle_order_count\nFROM retail.orders\nWHERE store_id = 1;",
        "SELECT COUNT(*) AS order_count, MIN(order_date) AS min_order_date, MAX(order_date) AS max_order_date\nFROM retail.orders\nWHERE store_id = 1;"
    ],
    "components": [
        {
            "type": "text",
            "content": "<p><strong>Sales comparison: accessible stores vs. Zava Retail Seattle</strong></p><p>Your accessible-store view is effectively a <em>single-store dataset</em> in practice: <strong>Zava Retail Bellevue</strong> generated all recorded sales, while <strong>Zava Retail Seattle</strong> has <strong>0 orders</strong> and <strong>$0.00</strong> sales.</p><ul><li><strong>Accessible stores overall:</strong> <strong>$8,611,540.81</strong> in sales from <strong>36,917 orders</strong> and <strong>125,496 units</strong>; average order value was <strong>$233.27</strong>.</li><li><strong>Zava Retail Seattle:</strong> <strong>$0.00</strong> sales, <strong>0 orders</strong>, <strong>0 units</strong>; earliest and latest order dates are both <em>null</em>.</li><li><strong>Share of accessible-store sales from Seattle:</strong> <strong>0.0%</strong>.</li><li><strong>Gap vs. accessible-store total:</strong> Seattle is down <strong>$8,611,540.81</strong> in sales and <strong>36,917</strong> orders.</li></ul><p><strong>What this means:</strong> there is no measurable sales activity in Seattle to compare against the broader accessible-store performance. The entire sales base is coming from Bellevue, so any head-office comparison should treat Seattle as an inactive or data-missing location rather than an underperforming one.</p><p><strong>Category context for the active business:</strong> the largest sales drivers across the accessible stores are <em>Power Tools</em> at <strong>$3,059,141.80</strong> and <em>Storage &amp; Organization</em> at <strong>$2,041,846.44</strong>, together contributing <strong>$5,100,988.24</strong> (about <strong>59.3%</strong> of the category sales shown).</p>",
